@* ===== Plan Your Trip: practical information ===== *@
<section class="section section--sand" id="plan">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="section-head">
            <span class="eyebrow">Plan your trip</span>
            <h2>Practical information</h2>
            <p class="lead">
                Armenia is easy to travel: safe, affordable and welcoming. Here is what you need to know before you go.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-grid">
            <article class="info-card" id="best-time">
                <h3>Best time to visit</h3>
                <p>Late spring (May–June) and autumn (September–October) are ideal: mild weather, clear skies and the best conditions for sightseeing and hiking.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Summer</strong> is hot in Yerevan and the lowlands, but pleasant at Lake Sevan and in Dilijan.</li>
                    <li><strong>Winter</strong> brings snow and skiing at Tsaghkadzor.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card" id="getting-there">
                <h3>Getting there</h3>
                <p>The main gateway is <strong>Zvartnots International Airport (EVN)</strong>, 12 km (7.5 mi) west of central Yerevan. Taxis and a shuttle bus connect the airport with the city in about 20–30 minutes.</p>
                <p>Overland entry is also possible from Georgia by train, bus or marshrutka.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card" id="getting-around">
                <h3>Getting around</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Marshrutka</strong> shared minibuses link Yerevan with every town — cheap and frequent.</li>
                    <li><strong>Taxi apps</strong>: Yandex Go and the local GG app are inexpensive and easy in Yerevan.</li>
                    <li><strong>Yerevan Metro</strong> and buses cover the city centre.</li>
                    <li><strong>Car rental</strong> gives the most freedom for monasteries and mountain roads.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card" id="visa">
                <h3>Visa</h3>
                <p>Armenia is very open to tourists. Citizens of the United States, EU/Schengen countries and many other nations can enter visa-free for extended tourist stays; others can obtain an e-visa online.</p>
                <p class="note">Rules and day-limits change — verify your nationality's requirements at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs (<a href="https://www.mfa.am/en/visa" rel="noopener">mfa.am/en/visa</a>) before you travel.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card" id="currency">
                <h3>Currency</h3>
                <p>The currency is the <strong>Armenian dram (AMD, ֏)</strong>. Cards are widely accepted in Yerevan; carry cash for markets, marshrutkas and villages. ATMs are everywhere in cities.</p>
                <p class="note">Exchange rates change constantly — check the Central Bank of Armenia (cba.am) or xe.com for today's rate.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card" id="language">
                <h3>Language</h3>
                <p>Armenian, written in its own unique 39-letter alphabet created by Mesrop Mashtots around 405 AD. Russian is widely understood, and English is common among younger people and in tourism.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><em>Barev</em> — hello</li>
                    <li><em>Shnorhakalutyun</em> (or simply <em>mersi</em>) — thank you</li>
                </ul>
            </article>

            <article class="info-card" id="safety">
                <h3>Safety</h3>
                <p>Armenia is generally very safe for tourists, with low crime and a strong culture of hospitality. Use normal precautions in the city and drive carefully on mountain roads.</p>
                <p class="note">Avoid the border areas with Azerbaijan and check your government's current travel advisory before your trip.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
